---
layout: layouts/base.njk
title: "Menu Board | The Café"
description: "In-store menu board for The Café. Not part of the public site."
pageCss: "/assets/css/menu-board.css"
bodyClass: board-body

# Unlisted, not private. It is off the nav, off the sitemap, and told not to
# be indexed — but this is a public repo on a public host, so anyone with the
# URL can open it. Nothing sensitive goes on this page for that reason.
noindex: true
robots: "noindex, nofollow"

# No nav, no footer, no construction banner, and nothing for main.js to do —
# this renders on a TV with no keyboard and no mouse.
noSiteJs: true
---
{#- ===========================================================
    /menu-board — the in-store screen

    Built for a 1920 x 1080 TV running a browser in fullscreen (F11) off the
    mini PC in the back. Same data as /menu and the printed tri-fold, laid
    out for someone reading it from across a room rather than from a phone
    at arm's length.

    HOW IT SCALES. There is no fixed 1920px canvas and no JavaScript. The
    root font-size is tied to the viewport (see menu-board.css) and every
    length on the page is in rem, so the layout is proportionally identical
    at 1080p, at 4K, and in a small window while you are checking it on a
    laptop. Nothing to configure on the mini PC beyond opening the URL and
    pressing F11.

    WHAT IS DELIBERATELY MISSING. No item descriptions: at the size they
    would have to be, nobody standing at the counter can read them, and a
    board that is too dense reads as noise. The board is names and prices —
    what a person scanning a wall needs — and the descriptions live on
    /menu and on the tri-fold for anyone who wants them.

    COLUMN ASSIGNMENT is by hand, below, not automatic. The four columns run
    roughly 20 lines each; a `columns: 4` flow would rebalance itself the
    moment an item is added and move half the menu to a different part of the
    wall overnight. Regulars learn where their order lives. If you add items,
    re-balance these lists on purpose — and PROOF IT at 1920x1080, because a
    column that overruns is drawn straight over the footer rather than
    clipped, and nothing in the build catches it.

    Soup sits in column 1 and the kids meals in column 2 for fit, not for
    meaning: those two are the only categories small enough to move without
    unbalancing everything else.
    =========================================================== #}

{% from "partials/diet-marks.njk" import dietMarks %}

{%- set breakfast = (menu.categories | where("id", "breakfast"))[0] %}
{%- set breakfastSides = (menu.categories | where("id", "breakfast_sides"))[0] %}
{%- set wraps = (menu.categories | where("id", "wraps"))[0] %}
{%- set sandwiches = (menu.categories | where("id", "sandwiches"))[0] %}
{%- set hotSandwiches = (menu.categories | where("id", "hot_sandwiches"))[0] %}
{%- set burgers = (menu.categories | where("id", "burgers"))[0] %}
{%- set salads = (menu.categories | where("id", "salads"))[0] %}
{%- set soup = (menu.categories | where("id", "soup"))[0] %}
{%- set kids = (menu.categories | where("id", "kids"))[0] %}
{%- set sides = (menu.categories | where("id", "sides"))[0] %}
{%- set drinks = (menu.categories | where("id", "drinks"))[0] %}

{%- macro category(c) %}
<section class="bcat">
    <h2 class="bcat-name">{{ c.name }}</h2>
    {%- if c.serving %}
    <p class="bcat-sizing bcat-serving">{{ c.serving }}</p>
    {%- endif %}
    {%- if c.sizing %}
    <p class="bcat-sizing">
        {%- for s in c.sizing %}{{ s.size }} {{ s.price | money }}{% if not loop.last %} &middot; {% endif %}{% endfor -%}
    </p>
    {%- endif %}
    {%- if c.items %}
    <ul class="bitems">
        {%- for item in c.items %}
        <li class="bitem">
            <span class="bitem-name">{{ item.name }}{% if menu.dietsConfirmed %}{{ dietMarks(item, menu.diets) }}{% endif %}</span>
            {#- No leader on a row with no price of its own: the salads are
                priced by size on the category line above, and dots running out
                to blank space read as a price somebody forgot to fill in. The
                same goes for an item priced by variant — its prices are on the
                rows underneath it. -#}
            {%- if item.price or item.prices %}
            <span class="bitem-fill" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            {%- endif %}
            {%- if item.price %}
            <span class="bitem-price">{{ item.price | money }}</span>
            {%- elif item.prices %}
            <span class="bitem-price">
                {%- for pr in item.prices %}{{ pr | money }}{% if not loop.last %} / {% endif %}{% endfor -%}
            </span>
            {%- endif %}
        </li>
        {#- Variants become their own rows, indented under the item. This board
            prints no descriptions, so an upgrade described in prose would be
            invisible here — the variant rows are how somebody standing at the
            counter learns the croissant costs more. -#}
        {%- for v in item.variants %}
        <li class="bitem bitem--variant">
            <span class="bitem-name">{{ v.size }}</span>
            <span class="bitem-fill" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="bitem-price">{{ v.price | money }}</span>
        </li>
        {%- endfor %}
        {%- endfor %}
    </ul>
    {%- endif %}
    {#- Build-your-own — see _note_picks in menu.json. Two short columns read
        faster from across a room than six rows all carrying the same price. -#}
    {%- if c.picks %}
    <div class="bpicks">
        {%- for p in c.picks %}
        <div class="bpick">
            <p class="bpick-label">{{ p.label }}</p>
            <ul class="bpick-list">
                {%- for o in p.options %}
                <li class="bpick-option">{{ o }}</li>
                {%- endfor %}
            </ul>
        </div>
        {%- endfor %}
    </div>
    {%- endif %}
</section>
{%- endmacro %}

<main class="board">

    <header class="board-head">
        <div class="board-mark">
            {% inlineSvg "/assets/thecafe.svg", "board-wordmark", site.name %}
        </div>
        <p class="board-tagline">Breakfast &amp; Lunch &middot; Catering &amp; Meal Prep</p>
        <p class="board-hours">{{ site.opening.hoursDays }} &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; {{ site.opening.opens.time }} {{ site.opening.opens.meridiem }} &ndash; {{ site.opening.closes.time }} {{ site.opening.closes.meridiem }}</p>
    </header>

    <div class="board-grid">
        <div class="board-col">
            {{ category(breakfast) }}
            {{ category(breakfastSides) }}
            {{ category(soup) }}
        </div>
        <div class="board-col">
            {{ category(wraps) }}
            {{ category(sandwiches) }}
            {{ category(kids) }}
        </div>
        {#- Everything off the griddle in one column. See _note_hot_cold in
            menu.json: the hot sandwiches belong beside the burgers, not with
            the deli case in the column to the left. -#}
        <div class="board-col">
            {{ category(burgers) }}
            {{ category(hotSandwiches) }}
            {{ category(drinks) }}
        </div>
        <div class="board-col">
            {{ category(salads) }}
            {{ category(sides) }}
        </div>
    </div>

    {#- Two rows, not one. The advisory is a full-width line of its own above
        the key/contact row: it is 150 characters, and as a third column
        between them it wrapped into an unreadable block in the corner. Laid
        out as an explicit block + row rather than by asking a flex child to
        claim a line, because that is a layout that cannot be misread. -#}
    <footer class="board-foot">
        <p class="board-advisory">{{ menu.healthNote }}</p>
        <div class="board-foot-row">
            {#- The key rides with the marks. While menu.json's `dietsConfirmed`
                is false there are no marks on this board, so a key explaining
                them would be explaining nothing. -#}
            {%- if menu.dietsConfirmed %}
            <p class="board-key">
                {%- for d in menu.diets %}<span class="diet-mark">{{ d.code }}</span> {{ d.name }} &nbsp;&nbsp;{% endfor -%}
                <span class="diet-mark">*</span> on request
            </p>
            {%- else %}
            <p class="board-key">Ask us about gluten free, vegetarian and low sodium options.</p>
            {%- endif %}
            <p class="board-contact">{{ site.phone }} &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; {{ site.social.website }}</p>
        </div>
    </footer>

</main>
